+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds persistent connections it operates itself and pushes each new event revision to the subscribed browsers, with no third-party realtime service in the path. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
